    "deck": "The Moonchild Tarot",
    "name": "Shadow Work",
    "suit": "Major Arcana",
    "keywords_up": "Illumination, balance, synergy, healing, acceptance\n\nWhat deeper habits are ready for my awareness? \n\nWhere is my shadow presenting itself the most?",
    "keywords_rev": "",
    "meaning_up": "Within each of us is a shadow self. A multifaceted, inner-vessel of the pains, traumas, fears, and suppressions we have experienced throughout life.\n\nVery often, we may think of the the shadow as something negative or malevolent, as the word itself conjures up themes of darkness or fear. But if we look a bit closer, we may find that our shadows are the ultimate liberators - acting as the most profound catalysts for aligning with our truth. Like the dark matter of our Universe, dreams and seeds of light may be cultivated here. Healing and creation may be birthed through cycles and doorways of revolutionary change.\n\nOur shadows can help anchor our own brilliance and self-worth, in teaching us to rise and transmute from the ashes that divide us from within. This may happen when we release our hidden burders, or remove the masks of the personas we continuously hide behind, allowing us to dive deeper within the waters of our heart.\n\nThe obscured aspects of our subconscious also show us where our thoughts or habits have become entrenched or addictive over time, which is helpful when looking at how and where we may need to make some profound changes in our life.\n\nWhen we are born, we are new to the world, and our shadows have yet to fully form. As we grow, we begin to see ourselves as separate and individual from the people around us, which eventually accumulates pieces of the identities that define us, and in the process - our shadows and egos take shape.\n\nWe then begin to judge and survey everything around us from our own unique lens: what is good, bad, right or wrong, and what we consider to be true and sacred in life.\n\nVery often, these standards are also reinforced by our family of origin, which can leave a lasting impression on our view of the world, prompting us to project our lens onto others over time.\n\nIn order to bring our hidden facets, qualities, or dreams to light, we can delve into 'shadow work' - a concept coined by the renowned psychologist Carl Jung - which describes the process of exploring our suppressed ideas, desires, feelings, traits, gifts, talents, or belief systems that we have either consciously, or unconsciously, hidden within ourselves. These may also be the facets of our human experience that are locked away, waiting to be reintegrated or healed.\n\nShadow work gives us an opportunity to explore the contrasts within our lives that have taught us the most profound lessons along the way.\n\nThe moments that redefine us as stronger, wiser, and softer through our struggles and shortcomings.\n\nWhen we recognize the raw, heartfelt strength we encompass as a result of how far we have been pushed.\n\nWhen we rise in courage or love through experiencing fear or heartache.\n\nWhen we take less for granted, after our sense of comfort or security has been challenged or reduced.\n\nThe shadow also shows us where we are harbouring suppressed dreams or inner creative urges that we have never had a chance to explore due to the internal or external struggles in our life that have held us back.\n\nThis can lead to fractures within our energetic health which can also be very depleting to our vitality and spirit.\nIf you feel like this may be true for you, it may be time to consider what you feel is missing, and how you can support these dreams or incomplete pieces. Take some moments to reflect with gentle care, by listening to what wants to come out or be expressed.\n\nThe shadow is here to teach us some of the greatest lessons we will ever experience. It also helps us understand our shifting ebb and flow in coming to terms with this part of ourselves, knowing that there isn't always a finish line or a solid answer to define. It illuminates a lifelong journey of inner-discovery, in realizing where we have hurt, fallen short, and grown at various crossroads. That it's okay to have bad days, make mistakes, and even get lost within those occasional dark nights of the soul. That we are beautiful and perfect in our human imperfections.\n\nIt's helpful to remember, too, that Shadow work is not a quick-fix or a one-size-fits-all medicine. Rather, it holds a very unique vibration for each individual and may be approached in many different ways. Perhaps your own experience with this work will involve reaching out for support or seeking some kind of deep-rooted healing.\n\nPerhaps it will include a daily form of introspection that you can explore at your own pace. The important thing to remember is that it's your own journey to take and that there are many ways to seek a comfortable practice with how you unveil its underlying light.",
